\section{Reduce a Failing Case Without Changing Its Meaning}

For the modular oracle, a reducer can work on typed syntax
instead of deleting source characters. Its input is a failing
program, concrete inputs, and a fixed target configuration.
Suppose the test requires exactly the deterministic unsigned
subset, a successful build, and a wrong returned word.
Protect the interface and the targeted operation shape with
an independent admissibility check. Every accepted edit must
strictly reduce a chosen size measure, such as the count of
syntax nodes, and retain the same class of mismatch.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Failing typed program $P$, fixed inputs $I$ and target
\Repeat
  \Let{Q}{P}
  \ForAll{strictly smaller candidates $R$ from $Q$}
    \To{Reject changes outside the oracle's defined subset.}
    \If{$R$ is admissible and still exercises the target feature}
      \Let{v}{\operatorname{interpret}(R,I)}
      \To{A compile failure is not a wrong returned value.}
      \If{compiling and running $R$ returns a word unequal to $v$}
        \Let{P}{R}
        \State Break
      \EndIf
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
\Until{$P=Q$}
\State \Return $P$
\end{sudocode}

For a harness self-test, use a toy faulty translator that
changes subtraction to addition. In an eight-bit unsigned
IR test at $a=255$, $b=1$, take
$\operatorname{sub}(\operatorname{add}(a,b),b)+0$.
The interpreter returns $255$; the bad translator returns
$1$. Deleting the final addition by zero keeps that mismatch.
Changing the input to $b=0$ instead makes both return $255$
and is not a valid reduction for the fixed test. Removing the
subtraction altogether also drops the operation that the test
is supposed to exercise.

At each accepted step the input, definedness, and discrepancy
are known, and the syntax size decreases, so a finite search
terminates. The result is locally irreducible under the edit
rules tried, not necessarily the smallest witness. Keep
diagnostic, timeout, and unsupported outcomes distinct from
wrong-value returns. If the oracle promises a wraparound
intermediate as part of the regression, check that premise
after each edit rather than inferring it from the existence
of any wrong output.

\section{Triage, Minimize, and Keep the Evidence}

The dossier's \emph{Semantic Fingerprints and Metamorphic Compiler Testing} generalizes this idea when absolute expected output is expensive: apply a transformation with a known observational relation and compare the resulting runs. For example, permutation preserves a pure modular sum, but does not necessarily preserve a trace that reports each operand as it is read. A fingerprint of normalized observations helps group failures, not prove equivalence; keep the original events and normalization rules for review. Munley, Jarmusch, and Chandrasekaran's \emph{LLM4VV} studies generating OpenACC validation tests and analyzing their outcomes. Generation can improve variety, but plausible-looking source and successful compilation are not independent checks of the assertions the test is meant to exercise.

On disagreement, save the seed, source and concrete inputs, compiler versions and options, observed results, and the oracle's premises. A reducer may remove code only while preserving a discrepancy under those premises: otherwise it can turn a valid miscompilation report into an undefined program or change the feature being tested. Replay the reduced case under the reference and the original toolchains, then retain it as a regression. If useful, submit the reduced transformation to a scoped translation validation check (Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-219}). Neither agreement on the regression corpus nor a high mutation score proves that all future translations are correct.
